.TH GETPAYLOAD 3 "June 2026" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
getpayload, getpayloadf, getpayloadl,
setpayload, setpayloadf, setpayloadl,
setpayloadsig, setpayloadsigf, setpayloadsigl \- access and set NaN payloads
.SH LIBRARY
Math library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.BI "double getpayload(double *" x );
.br
.BI "double getpayloadf(float *" x );
.br
.BI "double getpayloadl(long double *" x );
.PP
.BI "double setpayload(double *" res ", double " x ", int " payload );
.br
.BI "double setpayloadf(float *" res ", float " x ", int " payload );
.br
.BI "double setpayloadl(long double *" res ", long double " x ", int " payload );
.PP
.BI "double setpayloadsig(double *" res ", double " x ", int " payload );
.br
.BI "double setpayloadsigf(float *" res ", float " x ", int " payload );
.br
.BI "double setpayloadsigl(long double *" res ", long double " x ", int " payload );
.PP
Feature-test macro requirements (see
.BR feature_test_macros (7)):
these functions are declared when the implementation is compiled as
ISO C23 or later
.RB ( "__STDC_VERSION__ >= 202311L" ).
.SH DESCRIPTION
A NaN carries a
.I payload
in its mantissa bits.  These functions read and construct those
payloads.
.PP
The
.BR getpayload ()
function reads the value pointed to by
.I x
and, if it is a NaN, returns its payload (the mantissa bits below the
quiet bit) as a non-negative floating-point integer.
.PP
The
.BR setpayload ()
function constructs a
.B quiet
NaN whose payload is the integer
.I payload
and whose sign is taken from
.IR x ,
storing the result in
.IR *res .
.PP
The
.BR setpayloadsig ()
function is identical except that it constructs a
.B signaling
NaN.  If
.I payload
is 0, the implementation sets the least-significant mantissa bit so the
result is a NaN rather than an infinity.
.PP
The
.B f
and
.B l
suffixed variants operate on
.B float
and
.B "long double"
storage respectively.  The
.I payload
argument always has type
.BR int .
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR getpayload ()
returns the payload of
.I *x
as a non-negative floating-point integer.  If
.I *x
is not a NaN, it returns
.BR \-1 .
.PP
.BR setpayload ()
and
.BR setpayloadsig ()
return 0 on success, having stored the constructed NaN in
.IR *res .
They return a nonzero value (specifically
.BR \-1 )
if
.I payload
is negative or too large to fit the payload field of the format
(51 bits for
.BR double ,
22 bits for
.BR float );
in that case
.I *res
is
.B not
modified.
.SH ERRORS
No errors occur in the
.IR errno /
floating-point-exception sense.  These functions do not set
.I errno
and raise no floating-point exception; in particular, constructing or
reading a signaling NaN does not raise
.BR FE_INVALID .
An out-of-range payload is reported only through the nonzero return
value of
.BR setpayload ()
/
.BR setpayloadsig ()
described under RETURN VALUE.
.SH ATTRIBUTES
For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
.BR attributes (7).
.TS
allbox;
lb lb lb
l l l.
Interface	Attribute	Value
T{
.BR getpayload (),
.BR setpayload (),
.BR setpayloadsig (),
and their
.BR f /
.B l
variants
T}	Thread safety	MT-Safe
.TE
.SH STANDARDS
C23.
.SH HISTORY
Added in ISO C23 (§7.12.11), following the NaN-payload operations of
IEEE 754-2008.
.SH NOTES
The
.I payload
argument is an
.BR int .
The payload field of a
.B double
is 51 bits and that of a
.B "long double"
is 64 bits, both wider than a 32-bit
.BR int ;
payloads larger than
.B INT_MAX
therefore cannot be requested through this interface.
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program builds a quiet NaN with payload 42, then reads it
back:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    double n;

    if (setpayload(&n, 1.0, 42) != 0) {
        printf("payload out of range\en");
        return 1;
    }
    printf("isnan        = %d\en", isnan(n));
    printf("getpayload   = %.0f\en", getpayload(&n));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
The output is:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
isnan        = 1
getpayload   = 42
.EE
.RE
